# David-Smash's browser code reads no environment variables at run time. There
# is nothing to configure, no account to create and no key to obtain to play
# locally.
#
# One optional variable is read at build time: BUILD_ID, the name online
# players compare so that two different deploys refuse each other
# (next.config.ts). Left unset, a build is named after its commit, plus a hash
# of any uncommitted changes in this package. A production build with no git
# to ask has no name, and fails unless BUILD_ID is set: to a commit id, or to
# dev if a build that pairs with every other unnamed build is really wanted.
# Likewise a production build that cannot read this package's uncommitted
# changes (a failing git diff, an unreadable file) refuses rather than call the
# tree clean.
#
# The only settings live in the Cloudflare Worker (wrangler.jsonc), and two of
# them are secrets: the Cloudflare TURN key that lets players behind strict
# NATs connect. Without them the Worker hands out STUN only, which still covers
# LAN play and most home connections.
#
# Production — David sets them once, from this directory:
#   pnpm exec wrangler secret put TURN_KEY_ID
#   pnpm exec wrangler secret put TURN_KEY_API_TOKEN
#
# Local `pnpm preview` (wrangler dev) reads them from a git-ignored .dev.vars
# file next to this one, in this format:
# TURN_KEY_ID=
# TURN_KEY_API_TOKEN=
